00:11:43.720 --> 00:11:49.270
demonstrate really good soft skill, and they ended up
getting an offer. So when you get to that final stage,

00:11:49.270 --> 00:11:55.240
the company will index on both tech and soft skill. So
for the soft skills, I'm just going to touch on this

00:11:55.240 --> 00:12:02.140
really quickly. A lot of students didn't prepare for
this portion. And the question is usually the format of

00:12:02.140 --> 00:12:10.720
can you tell me about a time that you x? What did you
do? What was the end outcome? And a lot of people

00:12:10.720 --> 00:12:18.550
didn't come prepare for this. And a lot of students
ended up sharing like personal story about maybe a time

00:12:18.550 --> 00:12:24.760
that they overcome hardship, or overcome challenges and
things like that, as an interviewer, I can't document

00:12:24.760 --> 00:12:31.060
that, from a legal perspective, if you share anything
personal, I might just have to kind of acknowledge that

00:12:31.060 --> 00:12:36.520
in the interview setting like thank you for sharing
that. Do you have a different example? And usually,

00:12:36.520 --> 00:12:43.180
when I asked that question, or interviewers asked that
question, we want concrete example of a time that it

00:12:43.180 --> 00:12:51.580
actually happened and how you navigated that situation.
And 80% of the students that I talked to, they would

00:12:51.580 --> 00:12:58.870
give me hypothetical example of like, oh, if it
happened, I would do this. And I would just have to

00:12:58.930 --> 00:13:06.100
remind them like, Okay, did it happen? Can you tell me
something that actually happened. So prepare for those

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:12.250
example would be super helpful, I would say, keep in,
you know, in the back of your mind, like, prepare like

00:13:12.250 --> 00:13:17.770
five to seven examples. And then make sure that you
structure them in a STAR method. And that stands for

00:13:17.950 --> 00:13:24.850
situation, task, action, and results. Make sure that
you prepare and rehearse it a few times, so that you

00:13:24.850 --> 00:13:31.510
don't end up like rambling during the interview. And so
and then be prepared for follow up questions, too. This

00:13:31.510 --> 00:13:36.820
is another thing where, let's say ask that question.
Tell me about a time ABCD. And I just wanted to ask

00:13:36.820 --> 00:13:42.430
follow up questions. And the student just thought that
they did something wrong, or the answer wasn't correct.

00:13:42.460 --> 00:13:48.430
And they just completely like, shut down. So I've seen
that happen a lot. It's like it's very normal. The

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:54.730
interviewer just want to ask more follow up question,
just so that we can write clear feedback in order to

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:59.500
you know, make a case for you later, we think that
you're a strong candidate. So don't freak out when that

00:13:59.500 --> 00:14:07.060
happened. And then on the tech side, I mean, obviously
you leet code, but the thing is, if you have the

00:14:07.060 --> 00:14:14.200
cleanest solution, but you couldn't talk through it,
you probably wouldn't get a yes from the interviewer.

00:14:14.200 --> 00:14:20.770
So usually from an interviewer would look for A. that
this candidate at asked good clarifying questions. And

00:14:20.770 --> 00:14:28.090
the questions are usually really ambiguous. It's
designed that way, because they want you to ask those

00:14:28.090 --> 00:14:34.420
question first before you jump into problem solving
mode. And then before you even, after you ask a

00:14:34.420 --> 00:14:41.230
clarifying question, be sure to talk through your
thought process, and then be open to hints and

00:14:41.230 --> 00:14:47.530
feedback. So this is the biggest thing. We know that
you don't have a lot experience. We're gonna have to

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:53.110
coach you. We're going to have to mentor you, we're
going to have to develop you. And the biggest thing

00:14:53.110 --> 00:14:59.080
that we look for here is not the cleanest solution.
We're looking for how coachable you are, and a lot of

00:14:59.080 --> 00:15:04.870
the times that interviewer will try to give hints or
see if you know, if this candidate is stuck, are they

00:15:04.870 --> 00:15:10.510
proactive about asking for hints? Are they practical
about asking for help? And there are so many times when

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:16.150
the interviewer tried to give hints. And the student or
the candidate just didn't listen at all, and they just

00:15:16.150 --> 00:15:23.710
kept going. So then from an interviewer perspective,
they now raise an issue of, well, I may have an issue

00:15:23.710 --> 00:15:30.070
with coachability, because I tried to give him so many
hints and they didn't listen. So you don't need to have

00:15:30.070 --> 00:15:35.650
like the best answer, make sure that you communicate,
ask clarifying question, talk through your thought

00:15:35.650 --> 00:15:41.020
process, open to hints and feedback. So those are like
the three things that you always need to do in the

00:15:41.020 --> 00:15:42.000
technical interview.

